course: chemistry
lesson: 31
title: Metals and what they're good for
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Leave a metal spoon in hot soup and soon the handle is too hot to hold, while a wooden spoon stays cool. Metals pass heat along very well, and electricity too, which is why wires are copper inside their plastic coat.
explain: Metals are shiny, they ring when you strike them, and they can be bent and hammered into shape without shattering. Different metals are good at different jobs.
explain: Mixing metals makes alloys that are better than the pure metals: copper with tin makes bronze, and iron with a little carbon makes steel, strong enough for bridges.
explain: Most metals are dug out of rocks where they are joined to other elements and must be separated, which costs energy and scars the land. Only a few metals, such as gold, are found pure.
fact: Metals conduct heat well, so a metal spoon in hot soup gets hot while a wooden one stays cool.
fact: Copper conducts electricity very well, so electric wires are made of copper, while wood and plastic hardly let electricity through, which is why the wire has a plastic coat; copper pipes are also used because copper does not rust, though it slowly turns dull or green.
fact: Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin; it is harder than either metal, and the Bronze Age began over 5000 years ago.
fact: Steel is iron with a little carbon mixed in, which makes it much stronger; steel bridges need painting and regular checks for rust.
fact: Gold, silver and platinum are precious metals: rare, beautiful, and hardly reacting with air, although silver slowly tarnishes.
fact: Pure gold is so soft that it scratches easily, so most gold jewellery is gold mixed with other metals.
fact: Only a few metals are attracted by magnets: iron, nickel and cobalt, and steel because it is mostly iron; an aluminium drinks can is not.
fact: Some coins, such as British pennies made since 1992, are steel with a thin copper coat, so they stick to a magnet.
fact: Striking steel on flint knocks off tiny bits of steel made so hot by the blow that they burn as sparks; people lit fires this way long ago.
fact: Metals ring when struck because they pass vibrations on well; big bells are usually made of bronze.
fact: Most metals come from rocks called ores, dug out by mining, and mining can damage the land, which is one reason to recycle metals.
fact: Aluminium is about a third as heavy as steel of the same size, and titanium is strong and light, so both are used for light bike frames.
fact: Titanium is also used for hip replacements because the body accepts it well.
exercise: Why does the handle of a metal spoon in hot soup get hot? | Metals conduct heat well | The soup climbs up the spoon | Metals make their own heat | The steam only heats metal
exercise: Why are electric wires made of copper inside? | Copper conducts electricity very well | Copper is the cheapest metal there is anywhere | Copper is the lightest metal of all | Copper keeps the wire cool and dry
exercise: What is bronze made of? | Copper and tin | Iron and carbon | Gold and silver | Aluminium and zinc
exercise: Why did people in the Bronze Age make tools of bronze, not pure copper? | Bronze is harder than copper | Bronze is shinier than gold | Bronze was found pure in rivers | Bronze is lighter than wood
exercise: What is steel? | Iron with a little carbon mixed in | Copper with a little tin | Pure iron that has been polished until it shines | Aluminium that has been painted
exercise: Why do steel bridges need regular checks? | Steel can rust where the paint fails | Steel slowly melts in the hot summer sun | Steel turns soft in the rain | Steel becomes magnetic in winter
exercise: Why are gold, silver and platinum called precious? | They are rare, beautiful and hardly react | They are the heaviest things on Earth | They are the best metals for bridges | They are found in every garden
exercise: Why is most gold jewellery not pure gold? | Pure gold is soft and scratches easily | Pure gold is much too dark and dull in colour | Pure gold rusts in the rain | Pure gold is magnetic
exercise: Which of these does a fridge magnet stick to? | A steel fridge door | An aluminium drinks can | A gold ring | A copper pipe
exercise: Which metals are attracted by a magnet? | Iron, nickel and cobalt | Gold, silver and copper | Aluminium, tin and lead | Every metal there is
exercise: Why does steel struck on flint make sparks? | Tiny bits of steel get hot and burn | The flint is full of tiny bits of lightning | The stone has tiny matches inside | The air between them explodes
exercise: Why do bells ring for a long time when struck? | Metals pass vibrations on well | The bell is filled with air | The bell is hollow and empty | The rope keeps pulling it
exercise: Where do most metals come from? | Ores dug out of rocks | Trees and plants | The bottom of the sea | Rain that falls from clouds
exercise: Why is mining one reason to recycle metals? | Mining can damage the land | Mining makes metals too shiny | Mining is done only by robots | Mining makes metals weaker
exercise: Why are racing bikes often made of aluminium or titanium? | They are strong but light | They are heavy but soft | They never get scratched | They are the cheapest metals
exercise: Why is titanium used for hip replacements? | The body accepts it well | It is the heaviest metal | It glows in the dark | It is magnetic
test: Zoe: "magnets stick to all metal, that's what makes it metal". What's true? | Only a few metals, like iron, are magnetic | She's right, every metal is magnetic | Only gold and silver are magnetic | Metals are magnetic only when hot
test: Dad stirs the pan with a metal spoon and yelps: "bloody handle's red hot!" What should he use? | A wooden spoon, which stays cool | A thinner metal spoon | A wet metal spoon | A gold spoon, which never gets hot
test: If wires inside a house were made of wood instead of copper, what would happen? | Electricity would not flow through them | Every light in the house would be much brighter | The house would be warmer | Nothing at all would change
test: "pure gold is the best for rings, it's the hardest metal". What's true? | Pure gold is soft and scratches easily | Pure gold is the hardest metal | Pure gold can cut glass | Pure gold never bends at all
test: A British penny from 2010 sticks to your magnet. Why? | It is steel with a thin copper coat | Copper is very strongly magnetic indeed | It is a fake coin | The magnet is made of copper
test: In a video game, a hero builds a bridge from pure gold. What's wrong with that? | Gold is too soft and rare for a bridge | Gold rusts very fast in the rain and falls apart | Gold is too light for a bridge | Gold melts in the sunshine
test: Why would a Bronze Age smith add tin to copper? | To make a harder metal, bronze | To make the copper rust less | To make the copper magnetic | To make the copper float
test: "whack steel on a rock, sparks, so the rock's full of fire, right?" | No, tiny bits of hot steel burn as sparks | Yes, flint has lots of fire stored up inside it | Yes, the sparks are tiny stones | No, the sparks are from the air
test: Two bikes look the same; one is steel and one is aluminium. Which is lighter? | The aluminium one | The steel one | They weigh the same | It depends on the colour
test: If people recycled all metal and stopped throwing it away, what would happen? | Less mining would be needed | More mining would be needed | Metal would run out faster | Bikes would get heavier
exam: Identify the principal reason for which electrical conductors are customarily fabricated from copper. | Copper is an excellent conductor of electricity | Copper is the least dense of all metals | Copper is attracted strongly by magnets | Copper is the most abundant metal in rocks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises steel? | An alloy of iron with a small proportion of carbon | An alloy of copper with tin | A pure element found free in nature in large lumps | A compound of aluminium and oxygen
exam: It is given that a magnet is presented to a series of objects. Select the object which will be attracted. | A steel fridge door | An aluminium beverage can | A gold ring | A copper pipe
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which titanium is employed in hip replacements. | It is well tolerated by the human body | It is the most magnetic of metals | It is the softest of metals | It is luminous in darkness
